<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §01 -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The RL Bottleneck</div>
<h2>The Rollout Stage is Killing RL Training</h2>
<p>
  Reinforcement learning has become the core driver of LLM capability — GRPO, PPO, and async RL pipelines
  now underpin every frontier model from Qwen to DeepSeek. But the training loop has a brutal bottleneck:
  <strong>rollout</strong>. Generating trajectories token-by-token is slow, memory-bandwidth-bound, and
  constitutes 60–80% of total RL training time.
</p>
<p>
  Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) offers the natural fix: attach lightweight draft heads to the main model,
  propose 3–4 tokens simultaneously, verify them all in one forward pass. In standard inference, this
  yields 1.5–2× speedup. But in RL training — a very different regime — something goes wrong.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">MTP Acceptance Rate During RL Training (SWE-bench, Qwen3.5-3.6-Plus)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">acceptance rate per RL step, 3 MTP heads</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-degradation" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The chart above shows what actually happens during an RL run: all three MTP draft steps degrade
  continuously. Step 1 drops 1.2%, step 2 drops 2.6%, step 3 drops 3.5% — and because expected
  acceptance length is a <em>product</em> of per-step rates
  (E[L] = α₁ + α₁α₂ + α₁α₂α₃), the degradation compounds. By the end of training, the throughput
  gain from MTP has evaporated.
</p>

<p>
  The conventional explanation points to <strong>distribution mismatch</strong>: as RL weight updates move the
  backbone, the frozen draft heads fall behind. But Bebop's key finding is that this is <em>not</em>
  the dominant factor. The real culprit is <strong>policy entropy</strong>.
</p>

<div class="stat-row">
