<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 -->
<section id="s-entropy" data-code="entropy">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Entropy Bound</div>
<h2>High Entropy Makes Target-Only Sampling Impossible</h2>
<p>
  Under <strong>target-only sampling</strong> — the common default — a draft token is selected greedily
  as ŷ = argmax q(y), then accepted with probability p(ŷ). This seems reasonable: just check whether
  the draft's top choice is indeed likely under the target. But there's a hard bound:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Target-only acceptance rate</div>
  α<sub>TO</sub> = p(argmax<sub>y</sub> q(y))  ≈  max<sub>y</sub> p(y)  ≥  exp(−<span class="math-var">H</span>(p))
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;where H(p) = −Σ p(v) log p(v) is the target entropy</span>
</div>

<p>
  When the model is confident (low entropy), one token dominates — max p(y) is large, acceptance is
  high. When the model is uncertain (high entropy, as it is during RL exploration), probability spreads
  across many tokens — max p(y) plummets, acceptance falls linearly.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Target Distribution at Different Entropy Levels (12 vocabulary slots)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to change entropy level</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="viz-btns" id="entropy-btns">
      <button class="viz-btn active" data-entropy="low">Low H ≈ 0.3 (confident)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" data-entropy="mid">Medium H ≈ 0.8</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" data-entropy="high">High H ≈ 1.5 (exploring)</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-entropy" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.5rem;margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);font-size:.72rem;">
      <div>α<sub>TO</sub> = max p(y) = <strong id="ato-val" style="color:var(--purple-l)">—</strong></div>
