    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  During RL, the policy entropy naturally rises: the model must explore to discover better completions.
  The paper shows a <strong>clear negative linear relationship</strong> between entropy and acceptance rate
  across all model sizes and tasks. Crucially, this is not a draft quality problem — it's a fundamental
  ceiling imposed by the target distribution's shape.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 -->
<section id="s-rs" data-code="rs">
<div class="section-no">03 — Rejection Sampling</div>
<h2>Acceptance as Distributional Overlap</h2>
<p>
  The fix is conceptually elegant: switch from target-only to <strong>rejection sampling</strong>.
  Under rejection sampling, a draft token ŷ ~ q(·) is accepted with probability min(1, p(ŷ)/q(ŷ)).
  The expected acceptance rate is:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Rejection sampling acceptance rate</div>
  α<sub>RS</sub> = E<sub>ŷ~q</sub>[min(1, p(ŷ)/q(ŷ))]
  = <strong>Σ<sub>v</sub> min(p(v), q(v))</strong> = 1 − d<sub>TV</sub>(p, q)
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;This is exactly the distributional overlap (shaded green below)</span>
</div>

<p>
  The key difference: rejection sampling acceptance equals the <em>total overlap area</em> between
  p and q. When both distributions are uncertain (high entropy), both flatten together — and as long as
  the draft head tracks the target well, the overlap area stays large. The acceptance rate is no longer
  capped by entropy.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Target p (blue) vs Draft q (orange) — overlap (green) = acceptance rate</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">change entropy or draft training objective</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:.75rem">
      <div class="viz-btns" id="rs-entropy-btns">
        <button class="viz-btn active" data-rs-entropy="low">Low entropy</button>
        <button class="viz-btn" data-rs-entropy="high">High entropy</button>
      </div>
      <div style="width:1px;background:var(--border);margin:0 .25rem"></div>
      <div class="viz-btns" id="rs-mode-btns">
        <button class="viz-btn active" data-rs-mode="ce">CE-trained q</button>
        <button class="viz-btn" data-rs-mode="tv">TV-trained q</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-rs" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:2rem;margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);font-size:.72rem;">
      <div>α<sub>TO</sub> = <strong id="ato2-val" style="color:var(--amber)">—</strong></div>
      <div>α<sub>RS</sub> = <strong id="ars-val" style="color:var(--green)">—</strong></div>
      <div style="margin-left:auto;color:var(--muted)">green area = accepted tokens</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  But there's a catch: CE/KL-trained draft models still show entropy-dependent acceptance under
  rejection sampling. Even though the formula doesn't directly involve max p(y), the CE/KL training
  objective produces <strong>uniform per-token mismatch</strong> — errors spread evenly across the
  vocabulary. When entropy is high (large effective vocabulary size), these small errors accumulate
  proportionally to exp(H(p)), increasing the TV distance. This motivates the TV loss.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 -->
<section id="s-tvloss" data-code="tvloss">
<div class="section-no">04 — TV Loss</div>
<h2>Gradient Concentration: The Key to Entropy Invariance</h2>
<p>
  The root cause of CE/KL's entropy sensitivity: their gradients are <em>uniform</em> across the vocabulary.
  CE loss gradient at token j: ∂L_CE/∂z_j = q_j − p_j — this applies equal optimization pressure
  regardless of whether token j is high-probability or an irrelevant tail token.
</p>
<p>
  A capacity-limited draft model can only reduce a fixed total error budget. CE spreads this budget
  uniformly across all ~150,000 vocabulary tokens, including the vast tail of near-zero-probability
  tokens. As entropy rises, the effective vocabulary size grows as exp(H(p)), so the budget per
  relevant token shrinks — and TV distance grows proportionally.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">TV Loss vs CE Loss: Where Does the Gradient Go?</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the mechanism</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="tvloss-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">CE/KL: Uniform Gradient Spread</div>
          <div class="step-desc">CE gradient ∂L/∂z_j = q_j − p_j spreads across all vocabulary tokens equally. A low-probability token "swim" (p=0.001) gets nearly the same update magnitude as "the" (p=0.35). 99% of the optimization budget is wasted on the long tail.</div>
          <div class="step-code">grad_CE[j] = q[j] - p[j]  ← uniform |error|, ignores p[j]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">TV Distance = 1 − Acceptance Rate</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Rejection sampling acceptance rate exactly equals the distributional overlap. Minimizing TV distance directly maximizes acceptance rate — unlike KL divergence, which provides only an upper bound via Pinsker's inequality (d_TV ≤ √(D_KL/2)).</div>
          <div class="step-code">α_RS = Σ min(p(v), q(v)) = 1 - d_TV(p, q)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">TV Gradient: Probability-Proportional</div>
          <div class="step-desc">TV loss gradient ∂L_TV/∂z_j = −q_j·[1(q_j≤p_j) − S] is proportional to q_j. High-probability tokens get large updates; tail tokens get near-zero gradient automatically. The gradient is also bounded (|∂L/∂z_j| ≤ 1), ensuring stable training.</div>
          <div class="step-code">grad_TV[j] = -q[j] * (1[q[j] &lt;= p[j]] - S)  ← ∝ q[j]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Probability-Proportional Mismatch → Entropy Invariance</div>
          <div class="step-desc">When gradient ∝ q_j, the resulting per-token mismatch also scales with p(v): |q*(v) − p(v)| ≲ δ·p(v). The total TV distance then sums to δ/2 — independent of entropy! Even if the vocabulary explodes to 10,000 relevant tokens, each error is tiny relative to its probability weight.</div>
          <div class="step-code">d_TV(p, q*) ≤ δ/2·Σ p(v) = δ/2  ← entropy-invariant!</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  For multi-step MTP with γ heads, Bebop proposes the <strong>end-to-end TV loss</strong> that directly
  optimizes the expected acceptance length — accounting for the multiplicative structure:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">End-to-end multi-step TV loss</div>
  L<sub>e2e</sub> = 1 − <span class="math-var">1/γ</span> Σ<sub>j=1</sub><sup>γ</sup> Π<sub>i=1</sub><sup>j</sup> (1 − d<sub>TV</sub>(p<sub>i</sub>, q<sub>i</sub>))
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">&nbsp;&nbsp;Earlier steps appear in more product terms → naturally weighted more heavily</span>
</div>

<p>
  This is a <em>dynamic</em> weighting: since each α_i depends on current training state, the loss
  automatically shifts emphasis toward whichever step currently limits acceptance — no fixed decay
  factors or hand-tuned weights needed.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ §05 -->
<section id="s-slope" data-code="slope">
<div class="section-no">05 — Entropy Invariance</div>
<h2>95% Less Entropy Sensitivity</h2>
<p>
  The paper validates the entropy–acceptance relationship across all Qwen3.5, 3.6, and 3.7 training runs
  on reasoning, code, and agentic tasks. The result is a clean linear law in both cases:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Entropy-acceptance linear relationship</div>
  CE/KL-trained:  α ≈ a<sub>CE</sub> − <span class="math-var">b<sub>CE</sub></span> · H(p)  &nbsp;&nbsp;  slope b<sub>CE</sub> ≈ −1.68
  <br>
  TV-trained:     α ≈ a<sub>TV</sub> − <span class="math-var">b<sub>TV</sub></span> · H(p)  &nbsp;&nbsp;  slope b<sub>TV</sub> ≈ −0.06  &nbsp; (95% reduction)
</div>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Entropy vs Accept Length — CE Loss vs TV Loss</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle to compare training objectives</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="viz-btns" id="slope-btns">
      <button class="viz-btn active" data-slope="both">Both</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" data-slope="ce">CE/KL only</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" data-slope="tv">TV only</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-slope" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Each dot in the chart represents the mean entropy and accept length at one RL training step, pooled
  across model sizes and tasks. The TV line is nearly horizontal — the draft model has been made
  entropy-invariant by design. This is the key insight that makes pre-RL training sufficient: the TV-trained
  model doesn't need to be retrained as RL pushes entropy higher.
</p>
<p>
  Bebop's <strong>pre-RL strategy</strong>: train MTP heads once before RL with e2e TV loss and rejection
  sampling, then freeze them for the entire RL run. The decomposition analysis (Fig. 3 in the paper) shows
  that weight drift during RL (distribution mismatch) is negligible — under rejection sampling, the
  backbone's weight updates don't meaningfully change the TV overlap between draft and target.
</p>
</section>

